<InsightArticle title={e.title} description={e.description} date="2026-11-14" type={e.type as 'Analysis' | 'Explainer'} readTime="5 min" standfirst="Under the assumed limits here, increasing an answer from 1,000 to 2,000 tokens cuts the steady request ceiling from thirty to fifteen per minute. Adding workers cannot remove that output-token limit.">

<p>You have a request allowance and a queue of jobs. Launching enough workers to use the allowance seems straightforward until requests begin failing or waiting. Check the token allowances before adjusting concurrency. The largest request count printed on a limits page may not govern your workload.</p>
<p>Anthropic's <a href={e.sources[0].url}>rate-limit documentation</a>, read on 14 November 2026, distinguishes requests per minute, input tokens per minute and output tokens per minute. It also warns that bursts can exceed limits. We use hypothetical allowances below so the calculation is independent of a changing account tier.</p>
<h2>Translate each allowance into jobs</h2>
<p>Assume sixty requests per minute, 120,000 input tokens per minute and 30,000 output tokens per minute. Divide each token allowance by the amount one request uses. The smallest of the resulting job counts is the simple steady-state ceiling.</p>
<WorkedTable headers={["Assumed input/output per request","Request ceiling","Input ceiling","Output ceiling","Narrowest, requests/minute"]} rows={examples['ai-rate-limits-token-bottleneck']} caption="Hypothetical limits: 60 RPM, 120,000 input tokens/minute and 30,000 output tokens/minute. No provider tier is being quoted." />
<p>A small request reaches the request-count ceiling first. The middle workload reaches thirty jobs through both token allowances. The longer-output workload reaches fifteen through output, even though its input could support thirty. The arithmetic explains which resource deserves attention before you tune the queue.</p>
<p>These are planning ceilings, not observed delivery rates. Real requests vary in size and arrive at different times. The provider may estimate usage during processing, and the account may share capacity with another application. Preserve those conditions when comparing a prediction with a measured run.</p>
<h2>Concurrency measures something else</h2>
<p>Concurrency counts requests in flight. Throughput counts work completed over time. To plan a trial, estimate how many requests need to overlap to reach the desired completion rate at the observed request duration. Then increase cautiously and measure accepted completions, rather than assuming more simultaneous calls must help.</p>
<p>If the queue grows, record arrival rate and completion rate separately. A service completing steadily can still fall behind a larger incoming workload. The backlog is an application-level observation, not proof that the underlying model slowed down.</p>
<p>Keep queue waiting outside the provider-response timer and inside the user's total wait. That split lets you distinguish time waiting for permission to send a request from time after it was submitted.</p>
<h2>Use usage fields with their documented meaning</h2>
<p>Save the raw usage categories privately and derive a clearly labelled summary. Cached input, new input and generated output can be counted differently for billing and limits. Check the exact provider and model rules before subtracting anything from a token allowance.</p>
<p>The table deliberately assumes no caching. If you add it, recompute the relevant allowance using documented accounting and measured hit behaviour. A billing discount and a throughput benefit are separate claims. Neither follows simply from seeing the word “cache” in the configuration.</p>
<p>When a limit response gives a retry delay, respect it. Releasing the entire waiting queue at the same instant can create another burst. Use a bounded scheduling policy and keep the number of attempts visible, so retries do not masquerade as new demand.</p>
<h2>Track completed work under a stated load</h2>
<p>A useful run record includes account allowance checked date, workload token distribution, concurrency, queue delay, accepted completions and limit responses. Omit credentials and account identifiers from any public evidence. Publish synthetic workloads or aggregate counts when that is sufficient to explain the result.</p>
<p>The goal is a queue that finishes the required work within its deadline. First find the narrowest allowance. Then measure what the complete workflow can sustain under it.</p>
